│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adaptive_boundary_bias_patternsearch                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The original program simply returned random points, yielding a near-zero minimum triangle │
│                          │ area. My approach replaces that with a deterministic, vectorized stochastic hill-climb    │
│                          │ (pattern search with adaptive step sizes) directly on the true objective: the minimum     │
│                          │ triangle area normalized by the convex hull area. Key insights: (1) all C(13,3)=286       │
│                          │ triangle areas can be computed in a single vectorized cross-product operation, making the │
│                          │ objective cheap enough for thousands of evaluations per second; (2) the convex hull       │
│                          │ (computed with a lightweight monotone-chain hull) is the normalization region; (3)        │
│                          │ boundary-biased initializations (points on an ellipse/regular polygon with slight         │
│                          │ interior mixing) provide good starting basins, since optimal Heilbronn configurations     │
│                          │ push points toward the boundary; (4) a fixed seed plus a fixed iteration budget           │
│                          │ guarantees determinism. Single-point Gaussian perturbations with adaptive                 │
│                          │ shrinking/expanding steps give fine local refinement while remaining robust to the        │
│                          │ non-smooth min() objective.                                                               │
