│ patch_description        │ The original program returned random points (score ~0.01, min area ~0). Instead of random │
│                          │ placement, I implement a deterministic annealed hill-climbing optimizer: it starts from a │
│                          │ convex-position configuration (points near a circle, which avoids degenerate collinear    │
│                          │ triples), then iteratively perturbs single points with a geometrically decaying step      │
│                          │ size, accepting moves that increase the objective — the minimum triangle area normalized  │
│                          │ by the convex hull area. The objective is evaluated with a fully vectorized NumPy         │
│                          │ computation over all 286 point triples, making thousands of iterations fast. Key insights │
│                          │ used: optimal Heilbronn configurations tend toward convex position (interior points       │
│                          │ create small triangles), scale invariance lets the objective be the ratio                 │
│                          │ min-triangle-area / hull-area, and coordinate descent with annealing provides robust      │
│                          │ local refinement from a well-chosen structured start. A fixed seed guarantees             │
│                          │ reproducibility.                                                                          │
