│ patch_name               │ boundary_adam_softmin                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ Fundamentally different approach: instead of stochastic single-point hill climbing in     │
│                          │ full 2D space (26 free coordinates, where each move randomly disturbs the configuration), │
│                          │ we exploit the strong geometric prior that good Heilbronn configurations place points on  │
│                          │ the boundary of a convex curve (known ~n^-3 scaling for points on convex curves, beating  │
│                          │ interior placements). We parameterize all 13 points by angles on an ellipse (26 params:   │
│                          │ 13 angles + 2 semi-axes), making the hull area a smooth, cheaply computable function of   │
│                          │ the same parameters. We then optimize a smooth soft-min (LogSumExp with growing sharpness │
│                          │ p) of all 286 triangle areas divided by hull area, using Adam-style finite-difference     │
│                          │ gradient ascent with a decreasing learning rate. This gives directed, coordinated moves   │
│                          │ of all points simultaneously, converging far faster than blind random perturbation within │
│                          │ the time budget. Multiple deterministic angle seeds (uniform, staggered, perturbed,       │
│                          │ affine-scaled) with restarts and a final exact-score selection guarantee determinism and  │
│                          │ robustness.                                                                               │
