                        Patch Metadata - Gen 18/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ bottleneck_directed_moves_and_restarts                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ Expert insight on Heilbronn-type problems: the objective is bottleneck-driven — the       │
│                          │ minimum-area triangle improves only when its three vertices are moved to increase that    │
│                          │ specific triangle's area while not collapsing another. Pure random single-point moves     │
│                          │ waste most proposals. A directed move strategy that (a) identifies the current minimum    │
│                          │ triangle, and (b) moves one of its vertices along the direction that increases that       │
│                          │ triangle's altitude (perpendicular to the opposite edge, away from it), drastically       │
│                          │ improves proposal quality. Additionally, the 0.67→0.71 results show that multi-restart    │
│                          │ from the best configuration (basin hopping) is highly effective: local optima for n=13    │
│                          │ are numerous and shallow. Finally, since the score is the ratio min_area/hull_area,       │
│                          │ shrinking the hull can raise the ratio — so after optimization we apply a cheap affine    │
│                          │ "shrink-to-hull" rescale: translate points so the centroid of the hull is at origin, then │
│                          │ isotropically rescale by a factor slightly less than 1 and re-evaluate, keeping the       │
│                          │ rescaling only if it improves the exact score (deterministic line search). This exploits  │
│                          │ the fact that any convex rescaling of a valid configuration is still valid, and can turn  │
│                          │ a plateau in min-area into a gain in the normalized score.                                │
