│ patch_name               │ symmetric_triangle_anneal13                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ Key insight from the metrics: the benchmark score favors configurations where the convex  │
│                          │ hull itself is a fixed, maximal-area shape. Instead of scattering points in a unit square │
│                          │ (where the hull area shrinks and many near-collinear boundary triples appear), we anchor  │
│                          │ three points at the vertices of a unit-area equilateral triangle, so the hull area is     │
│                          │ exactly 1 and min_area_normalized equals the raw minimum triangle area. The remaining 10  │
│                          │ points are optimized with a deterministic, seeded annealing/perturbation scheme           │
│                          │ exploiting 3-fold symmetry in the initialization. All 286 triangle areas are computed     │
│                          │ with precomputed triple indices in a single vectorized NumPy operation. Points are        │
│                          │ handled in barycentric coordinates w.r.t. the triangle and projected (clipped to          │
│                          │ nonnegative, renormalized) so they always stay inside the convex region. An adaptive      │
│                          │ step-size random-perturbation refinement with a lexicographic tie-break (larger minimum   │
│                          │ area wins) drives the minimum triangle area up, targeting a normalized value near or      │
│                          │ above the benchmark constant 0.0309.                                                      │
