│ patch_description        │ Crossover of the two best implementations. From the current program (score 0.70) we keep: │
│                          │ (a) the fast incremental area updates — moving one point only affects its 66 triangles,   │
│                          │ so we cache all 286 areas and update only the affected slice; (b) the structured          │
│                          │ ring/disk initializations (ring of 12 + center, 13-ring, 7+6 double ring, etc.) which are │
│                          │ far better baselines than random disks; (c) the greedy sigma-decay descent with restarts. │
│                          │ From the second program (score 0.52) we borrow: (a) coordinated moves on the three        │
│                          │ vertices of the current bottleneck (minimum-area) triangle — these escape local optima    │
│                          │ that single-point moves cannot; (b) the soft bottleneck objective (mean of the smallest k │
│                          │ triangle areas) for occasional uphill acceptance, allowing annealing-like escape from     │
│                          │ poor basins; (c) the final strict greedy polish phase focused on the bottleneck triangle. │
│                          │ The hybrid runs greedy incremental descent (cheap due to caching), injects coordinated    │
│                          │ bottleneck-triangle moves periodically, and finishes each restart with a strict polish.   │
│                          │ This should strictly dominate both parents: better objective per unit time (incremental   │
│                          │ updates ~5x faster evaluation) plus better basin-escape (coordinated + soft moves), all   │
│                          │ under a deterministic time budget.                                                        │
