│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ incremental_triangle_eval_speedup                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The main cost per annealing step is a full O(286) triangle scan plus a Python-loop convex │
│                          │ hull. Implement the incremental scheme: precompute, for each vertex i, the indices of the │
│                          │ 66 triangles containing i and the 220 not containing i. When vertex i moves, only the 66  │
│                          │ affected areas change; the min over the other 220 can be cached along with the hull area  │
│                          │ (only recompute the hull when the moved vertex was a hull vertex). This gives roughly a   │
│                          │ 4-10x per-move speedup, which converts directly into many more annealing moves within the │
│                          │ 3s budget — the proven lever for score gains. Since a move only changes one point, we     │
│                          │ cache `min_rest` (min area over triangles not containing i) and `hull_area`; the          │
│                          │ candidate's min triangle area is min(min_rest, min over 66 affected triangles).           │
│ num_applied              │ 3                                                                                         │
