│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ fix_incremental_scoring_add_basin_kicks                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ All prior programs plateau at min_area_normalized = 0.02. Two problems in the current     │
│                          │ code: (1) `_point_outside_hull` always returns True ("conservative"), and it's actually   │
│                          │ called incorrectly (indexes `cand` with an interior index but never receives the moved    │
│                          │ vertex index), so accepted moves get exact recomputes via a wrong path while rejected     │
│                          │ moves use approximate hull areas that may never update — the cached `hull_a` can go stale │
│                          │ and corrupt the score silently. (2) The annealer only ever moves a single vertex at a     │
│                          │ time with adaptive sigma, which collapses to tiny local steps and cannot escape the       │
│                          │ incumbent basin — this is exactly why every program is stuck at 0.02.                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: (a) replace the acceptance block with an exact score recomputation on every accepted │
│                          │ move (full 286-triangle numpy evaluation + monotone-chain hull is only microseconds, so   │
│                          │ correctness costs nothing while the incremental min-over-rest still screens candidates    │
│                          │ cheaply); (b) replace `_point_outside_hull` with a proper point-in-convex-polygon test;   │
│                          │ (c) add a basin-hopping mechanism: when no improvement for several rounds, kick 3-4       │
│                          │ randomly chosen vertices simultaneously at sigma ~0.14 from the best-known configuration, │
│                          │ then resume local annealing — coordinated multi-vertex kicks are far more likely to jump  │
│                          │ between basins than isotropic single-vertex noise, and the strict best-seen bookkeeping   │
│                          │ guarantees the returned result never regresses.                                           │
