╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ incremental_triangle_scoring                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The main cost in `_hillclimb` is the full `_score` call (286 triangle areas + Python-loop │
│                          │ hull area) for every candidate, even though ~75%+ of moves perturb a single vertex. For a │
│                          │ single-vertex move only 66 of the 286 triangles change area; the other 220 can be read    │
│                          │ from a cached array. Similarly, if the moved vertex is not on the convex hull, the hull   │
│                          │ area is unchanged and the Python monotone-chain hull computation can be skipped entirely. │
│                          │ This edit adds precomputed per-vertex triangle masks and an incremental scoring helper,   │
│                          │ and rewrites `_hillclimb` to keep a cached `base_areas` array + cached hull area/hull     │
│                          │ vertex set, updating only the 66 touching triangles for single-vertex moves (full         │
│                          │ recompute only for coordinated 3-vertex moves and when a hull vertex moves). This roughly │
│                          │ 4x speedup in the inner loop converts directly into more hillclimb iterations and         │
│                          │ restarts within the fixed 3s budget, which is the dominant driver of the achieved         │
│                          │ min-area.                                                                                 │
│ num_applied              │ 3                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0126                                                                                   │
