│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adam_softmin_optimizer                                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The key observation from the prior programs: all runs plateau at min_area ≈ 0.02 while    │
│                          │ the best program (score 0.71) used the largest time budget (7s). Random hill climbing     │
│                          │ with single-point perturbations is extremely inefficient in 26 dimensions. A much         │
│                          │ stronger approach is a smooth surrogate of the min-area objective optimized by Adam with  │
│                          │ exact analytical gradients: use a soft-min (negated LogSumExp) over the 286 triangle      │
│                          │ areas plus a term lifting the k smallest areas (so the bottom of the area distribution    │
│                          │ rises together rather than one triangle at a time), and a smooth surrogate for the hull   │
│                          │ area in the denominator. The full gradient of every triangle area w.r.t. all 13 points is │
│                          │ computable in closed form and vectorized over all 286 triangles, so each Adam step costs  │
│                          │ only microseconds — thousands of steps per seed within a 7s budget. We keep a strict      │
│                          │ min-area evaluation for the true objective (tracking the best feasible configuration      │
│                          │ seen), add a final bottleneck-targeted polish, and enlarge the time budget to 7s since    │
│                          │ the 0.71-scoring program shows more time pays off. Seeds are diversified (rings,          │
│                          │ ellipses, 3-fold symmetric configurations known to be good for Heilbronn).                │
