├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ vectorized_polish_incremental_scoring                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The polish loop is the main consumer of the remaining budget, but each candidate move     │
│                          │ recomputes all 286 triangle areas plus a full Python monotone-chain hull. Instead,        │
│                          │ precompute a per-point triangle index map (`tri_map` = the 66 triangles containing vertex │
│                          │ i), cache the vector of 286 areas, and on each candidate move recompute only the 66       │
│                          │ affected triangles. Also replace `_rescale_unit_hull` per candidate (which recomputes     │
│                          │ hull) with a cheap scale correction only when the moved point is on the hull; for         │
│                          │ interior points the hull area is unchanged, so the normalization and the cached untouched │
│                          │ areas can be reused directly. Since scores are scale-invariant under uniform scaling, we  │
│                          │ can simply compute the candidate min-area ratio without rescaling at all. This converts   │
│                          │ the polish's wall-clock per move from ~O(286 + hull) to ~O(66) and dramatically increases │
│                          │ iterations within the 2.8s budget, letting the bottleneck-targeted hill climb converge    │
│                          │ much deeper (prior generations showed quality scales directly with polish iterations).    │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
